Abstract
The performance of energy storage materials is substantially dependent on their nanostructures. Herein, Ni-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate metal-organic frameworks (Ni-BTC MOFs) with different morphologies are controllably synthesized using a facile solvothermal method by simply adjusting the solvent and their electrochemical performance as an anode material for lithium-ion batteries is thoroughly investigated. Among the synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs with different morphologies, a hierarchical mesoporous flower-like Ni-BTC MOF (Ni-BTCEtOH) assembled from two-dimensional nanosheets shows the best electrochemical properties including a high capacity of 1085 mA h g-1 at 100 mA $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{358\mA\h\g^{-1}}$ at $5000\ \mathrm{mA}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ), excellent cycling stability at 1000 mA g for 1000 cycles, and great rate performance, which is superior to most of the reported MOF-based anode materials for lithium-ion batteries. The outstanding electrochemical performance of Ni-BTCEtOH is originated from its unique and stable hierarchical mesoporous morphology with a high specific surface area and improved electrical/ionic conductivity. Moreover, our study demonstrates that the charge-discharge mechanism of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode involves the insertion/extraction of Li ions to/from the organic moieties in Ni-BTCEtOH during the charge-discharge process without the direct engagement of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . This work highlights that the nanostructure design is an effective strategy to obtain promising energy storage materials.
Keywords: Ni-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate; morphology tuning; flower-like; lithium-ion batteries.
## 1. Introduction
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been considered as a promising energy storage system for portable electronic devices and hybrid electric vehicles because of their high reversible capacities and high energy densities [1-4]. In order to further improve the energy density of the next-generation LIBs, novel anode materials with high capacities as well as ideal cycling stability are necessary to replace the currently commercial graphite anode, which has a low theoretical specific capacity of $372~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [5, 6]. Up to now, various kinds of anode materials have been reported as promising alternatives for graphite due to their high theoretical capacities, such as metal oxides and alloys [7-9]. However, such anode materials usually suffer from severe morphology change and/or volume expansion during the charge-discharge process, which leads to inferior cycling stability. Although carbon coating and properly controlling the particle size could, to some extent, enhance the stability of these anode materials by accommodating their volume expansion during the charge-discharge process, they could not be effective if the morphology of these anode materials significantly changes. Therefore, the development of anode materials with structures that can be maintained during the charge-discharge process is of great significance.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), consisting of metal ions and organic ligands, have attracted increasing attention in gas storage [10, 11], drug delivery [12, 13], catalysis [14, 15], and sensors [16, 17]. Recently, some MOFs with high specific surface areas (due to high porosity) have been applied as anode materials for LIBs [18, 19], which could shorten the diffusion length of Li ions and improve their specific capacities during the charge-discharge process. Up to now, numerous MOFs have been synthesized and used as anodes for LIBs, such as $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ [20], MIL-53(Fe) [21], Ni2(1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxylate) [22], and Mn-(tfbdc)(4,4-bpy) $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ [23]. However, these MOF-based anode materials still have some problems, such as a low capacity, inferior rate performance, and terrible cycling stability, which largely hinder their further applications in LIBs. Wang group fabricated a $\mathrm{\small{[Fe_{3}O(B D C)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}(N O_{3})]_{n}}}$ (Fe-MIL-88B) nanorod electrode that showed a high capacity of $744.5\mathrm{\mA\h\g^{-1}}$ at $60\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ , but it only remained a low capacity of $133.4~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ [24]. Zheng et al. showed that the capacity of an aluminum fumarates (Al-FumA) MOF electrode could be stable for 100 cycles at $37.5\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ , but the capacity of that electrode was low $(392\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{~h~g^{-1})~}$ [19]. The low capacities and poor cycling stability of the MOF-based LIB electrodes are mainly due to the following reasons. (i) The MOF-based LIB electrodes usually store Li ions following the conversion mechanism during the charge-discharge process, which involves relatively large structural changes and therefore results in terrible cycling stability. $(i i)$ The as-synthesized MOFs are usually solid particles with low specific surface areas and porosities. These features are not suitable for Li-ion diffusion, leading to low capacities and stability. (iii) The electrical conductivities of MOFs are usually low, which also devastates their electrochemical performance. Therefore, designing MOFs with suitable morphologies, large specific surface areas, high porosities, excellent electrical conductivities, and proper Li-storage mechanisms (e.g., the intercalation/deintercalation mechanism) could be a promising way to improve the Li storage performance of the MOF-based anodes [19, 24]. However, the exploration of suitable transition metals and organic ligands for the construction of such MOFs is still a challenge.
Choosing proper organic ligands to coordinate with metal ions and build appropriate networks is of great significance for synthesizing MOF-based anode materials following the intercalation/deintercalation mechanism (e.g., Mn-BTC [25] and Zn(IM)1.5(abIM)0.5 [26]) because the insertion-extraction of Li ions mainly occur at the organic ligands. Previous work showed that MOFs with 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (BTC3-) as the ligands (e.g., Mn-BTC [25] and Cu-BTC [27]) usually obey the intercalation/deintercalation mechanism when used as the anode materials for LIBs. In addition, properly choosing central metal ions to coordinate with ligands is also critical for obtaining the MOF-based anode materials with suitable morphologies for rapid diffusion of Li ions. Among various MOFs composed by transition metals and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}.$ , Ni-BTC exhibits a relatively large specific surface area, stable three-dimensional (3D) porous structure (with calculated pore diameters in the range of $20{-}25\mathring\mathrm{A}\big\rangle$ ), good electrolyte accessibility, and high diffusion rate for Li ions [28, 29]. Furthermore, many reports have proved that some specific morphologies such as nanosheets can effectively shorten the diffusion distance of Li ions, which can largely improve the electrochemical performance of MOFs [19, 30].
Herein, we systematically studied the morphology-dependent performance of Ni-BTC MOFs as anode materials for LIBs. A unique hierarchical flower-like Ni-BTC assembled from two-dimensional (2D) Ni-BTC nanosheets has been synthesized by a simple solvothermal method. By adjusting the reaction solvent during the synthesis (i.e., N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) or absolute ethanol (EtOH) or DMF/EtOH), Ni-BTC MOFs with different morphologies (e.g., solid microspheres) can also be synthesized. Among them, the optimized flower-like Ni-BTCEtOH (i.e., the Ni-BTC MOFs synthesized using EtOH as the reaction solvent) shows an ultrahigh capacity $(1100\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ at $200\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ after 100 charge-discharge cycles) and robust cycling stability, which is superior to most of the reported MOF-based anode materials for LIBs. The excellent electrochemical performance of the Ni-BTCEtOH can be attributed to its flower-like morphology with a large surface area $(551~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , high electrical conductivity, stable structure, and proper charge-discharge mechanism (i.e., the intercalation/deintercalation mechanism).
## 2. Experimental Section
## 2.1 Material synthesis
The Ni-BTC MOFs were prepared through a simple solvothermal method. For the synthesis of Ni-BTCEtOH, 4.38 mmol of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 2.44 mmol of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{3}\mathrm{\bf{B}\mathrm{TC})}$ were dissolved in $60~\mathrm{mL}$ of absolute ethanol. After being stirred for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , the obtained homogeneous solution was transferred into a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ autoclave with a Teflon lining and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the resultant green precipitate was collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol, and then dried at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12{\mathrm{~h}}.$ . Similarly, the Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH were synthesized following the same procedure by using DMF and DMF/EtOH ${(1{:}1\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v})}$ as the solvent, respectively.
## 2.3 Electrochemical measurements
The as-prepared samples, super P carbon, and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) were mixed in the weight ratios of 70:20:10 by utilizing N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) as the solvent. The mixture was cast onto a copper foil by utilizing the doctor-blade technique. The Cu foil loaded with the active material was transferred to a vacuum oven and dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . Then, the loaded Cu foil was cut into disk electrodes (with a diameter of $1.2{\mathrm{cm}}{\mathrm{,}}$ ). The mass loading of the active material on each of the disk electrodes was $1.4\mathrm{-}1.6~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . To assemble the CR2016 coin-type cells, a prepared disk electrode, a solution of $1.0\mathrm{~M~LiPF}_{6}$ in a mixture of ethylene carbonate and diethyl carbonate (with a volume ratio of 1:1), and a piece of lithium foil were served as the working electrode, electrolyte, and counter electrode, respectively. The galvanostatic charge-discharge measurements were conducted on a LAND CT2001C battery test system from 0.01 to 3.0 V (vs. Li/Li+) at room temperature. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements of the cells were performed on a CHI660D electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chenhua Instruments Co.) at a scan rate of $0.2~\mathrm{\mV~\s^{-1}}$ . The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted on an EG&G Princeton Applied Research PARSTAT2273 potentiostat/galvanostat in the frequency range from $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ to study the charge transfer characteristics of the samples. The half-cells were undergone charge-discharge at a low rate of $100\ \mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ for 10 cycles before the EIS measurements.
## 3. Results and discussion
Three Ni-BTC MOFs (i.e., Ni-BTCDMF, Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH, and Ni-BTCEtOH) were synthesized via a simple hydrothermal method using DMF, DMF/EtOH (1:1, v/v), and EtOH as the solvent, respectively (Fig. 1), to study the effect of the solvent on the morphologies of the synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs. The optical photographs (insets in Fig. 2a) show that these three Ni-BTC MOFs are light green powder. The crystal structures of the as-synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs were studied by XRD. As shown in Fig. 2a, the XRD patterns of the three Ni-BTC samples show nearly identical diffraction peaks at the 2θ angles of 9.16, 9.84, 10.34, 11.22, 11.94, 12.20, 13.14, 14.64, 16.38, 17.26, and $18.46^{\mathrm{o}}$ , matching well with the simulated XRD pattern of Ni-BTC. It is noteworthy that the crystallinity of the synthesized Ni-BTC in this work is much higher than that of many Ni-BTC MOFs in previous reports [25, 31]. According to previous work, DMF might coordinate with metal ions [30]. For example, it has been reported that the DMF molecules could partially replace BTC3- to form a 3D porous structure with metal ions such as $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (Fig. A.1) [27, 32]. However, no extra diffraction peaks are observed in the XRD patterns of the synthesized Ni-BTC samples in this study, indicating that the DMF (if any) involved in the coordination reaction has no obvious effect on the crystalline structure of the synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs. The FT-IR spectra of Ni-BTCEtOH and $1,3,5{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ are shown in Fig. 2b to further study the chemical structure of the as-synthesized $\mathrm{Ni-BTC_{EtOH}}$ . The characteristic bands of the nonionized carboxyl groups in 1,3,5-BTC (i.e., $\nu_{\mathrm{OH}}{=}3088\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $\scriptstyle\mathtt{v_{C=O}=1720}\ \mathtt{c m}^{-1}$ , and $\delta_{\mathrm{C=0}}{=}535~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\cdot$ are absent in the FT-IR spectrum of $\mathrm{Ni{-}B T C_{\mathrm{EtOH}}[\cdot}$ 33]. In addition, the new bands appearing in the regions of $1618{-}1571~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1438{\cdot}1369~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the spectrum of Ni-BTCEtOH can be attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of -COO-, respectively [19]. Meanwhile, the new band at $761\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the spectrum of Ni-BTCEtOH can be assigned to the ring-out-of-plane vibration of the 1,3,5-substituted benzene core of the organic ligands [20]. The absence of the characteristic peak of hydroxyl groups (at ${\sim}3600~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) demonstrates that EtOH is not incorporated in the $\mathrm{Ni-BTC_{EtOH}}$ [24]. The differences between the bonding environments of Ni-BTCEtOH and 1,3,5-H3BTC indicate that $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ (in Ni-BTCEtOH) has coordinated with 1,3,5-H3BTC and formed the Ni-BTC MOFs. The FT-IR spectra of Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH (Fig. A.2) show similar results as that of Ni-BTCEtOH except for that extra peaks corresponding to the stretching vibrations of C-N (at ${\sim}1120~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ appear. In addition, the C-N peak intensity of Ni-BTCDMF is stronger than that of Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH. These results suggest that a certain amount of DMF molecules have coordinated with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in both Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH [34].
XPS was used to further study the surface chemical states of the as-synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs. As shown in the survey spectra of those three samples (Fig. 3a), the peaks of Ni (2p, 3s, and 3p), C 1s, and O 1s can be observed. The peaks in the high-resolution Ni 2p XPS spectrum of Ni-BTCEtOH at 856.1 and $873.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 3b) can be ascribed to $\mathrm{Ni}~2\mathrm{p}^{3/2}$ and $2{\mathfrak{p}}^{1/2}$ , indicating that Ni mainly exists as $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in Ni-BTCEtOH [35, 36]. Meanwhile, two satellite peaks at 861.4 and 879.7 eV are observed, which are well matched with the peaks of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in reported work [35, 36]. As for Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH, similar peaks of Ni 2p are also observed (Fig. A.3a and A.3b), suggesting that Ni also mainly exists as $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in them. In addition, the N 1s peak is observed in Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH (Fig. 3a). The N 1s XPS spectra of Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH (Fig. A.3c and A.3d) prove the existence of Ni-N-O or/and Ni-O-N bonds and further suggest the coordination of DMF with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH [37]. The TGA curve of Ni-BTCEtOH (Fig. 3c) undergoes a three-stage weight loss. The first two weight losses in the temperature ranges of $20–120\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}$ and $120{-}337^{\mathrm{~o}}\mathrm{C}$ are $13.2\%$ and $11.5\%$ , respectively, which can be assigned to the release of water and other impurities [38]. The third weight loss $(47.9\%)$ occurring at around $337\ ^{\mathrm{~o~}}\mathrm{{C}}$ could be assigned to the decomposition of Ni-BTCEtOH MOFs. As shown in Fig. A.4a, the TGA curves of Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH both show similar results. Meanwhile, the XRD patterns (Fig. A.4b) of the three Ni-BTC MOFs after calcination at $250~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $2\mathrm{{h}}$ show no obvious change compared to the XRD patterns (Fig. 2a) of the as-synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs, indicating that the structure of the Ni-BTC MOFs can maintain stable at quite high temperatures.
The morphologies of the as-prepared three Ni-BTC samples were studied by SEM (Fig. 4). When only DMF is used as the solvent, the Ni-BTCDMF displays a spherical morphology with an average particle size of $2{-}3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. 4a and 4b). Besides, the surface of the Ni-BTCDMF spheres is smooth. After adding EtOH into DMF (with a volume ratio of 1:1), the surface of Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH particles becomes corrugated (Fig. 4c and 4d). Interestingly, when EtOH completely replaces DMF as the solvent, the obtained Ni-BTCEtOH shows a hierarchical flower-like morphology (Fig. 4e) with a particle diameter of $2{\cdot}3~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ . As shown in the magnified SEM view in Fig. 4f, the hierarchical flower-like Ni-BTCEtOH is constructed of interwoven nanosheet-like subunits. The detailed morphology and structure of the Ni-BTCEtOH were further elucidated by TEM. Fig. 4g-i describe a well-defined flower-like structure for Ni-BTCEtOH. The “flowers” are assembled from 2D nanosheets with a thickness of tens of nanometers, which are expected to facilitate the Li-ion and electron transportation and electrolyte wetting. The high-angle annular dark field (HAADF) image of an individual particle of Ni-BTCEtOH (Fig. 4j) also suggests that the flower-like morphology of Ni-BTCEtOH is indeed assembled with numerous nanosheets. The corresponding elemental mappings (Fig. 4k-m) further confirm the existence and uniform distribution of Ni, C, and O in Ni-BTCEtOH.
The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms and pore size distribution of the three Ni-BTC MOFs are shown in Fig. A.5. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of the three samples show a typical Langmuir type IV characteristic with a slight hysteresis loop at high pressures [39, 40]. The BET specific surface area of the Ni-BTCEtOH is up to $551\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is much higher than that of Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH $(252~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH $(198~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . The high BET surface area for Ni-BTCEtOH could be ascribed to its flower-like morphology, which will be discussed later. The pore size distribution in Fig. A.5 was determined by using the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method. The pore sizes of Ni-BTCEtOH are mainly between 3.02 and $3.98~\mathrm{nm}$ . The pore size of Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH and Ni-BTCDMF centers at 2.58 and $2.28~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The broader size distribution of the pores in Ni-BTCEtOH can be attributed to the spaces between the nanosheets. Such a mesoporous structure with a high specific surface area could provide more lithium storage sites, accommodate the strain/stress during the cycling, and improve the electrode/electrolyte contact, which are expected to substantially improve the electrochemical properties of Ni-BTCEtOH as an anode material for LIBs.
To study the effect of the morphology on the electrochemical properties of Ni-BTC MOFs, the performance of Ni-BTCDMF, Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH, and Ni-BTCEtOH as anodes in LIBs was investigated in the voltage range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{V}$ at a current density of $200\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ . Fig. 5a shows the cycling performance of these samples. In the first cycle, the discharge capacities for Ni-BTCDMF, Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH, and Ni-BTCEtOH are 1780, 1800, and $1913\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively. During cycling, the capacities of all three samples appear a slight increase tendency because of the activation process of the electrodes, which has been extensively reported for MOF-based materials [19, 24, 25]. After 100 cycles, capacities of 519, 667 and $1085~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are obtained for Ni-BTCDMF, Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH, and Ni-BTCEtOH, respectively. Fig. 5b shows the coulombic efficiencies of the three samples at $200\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 100 cycles. The Ni-BTCDMF, Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH, and Ni-BTCEtOH display initial coulombic efficiencies of $39.6\%$ , $43.7\%$ , and $52.7\%$ , respectively, which are higher than those of many MOF-based anodes [24, 25]. The irreversible capacity loss in the first cycle may be ascribed to the formation of a solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) film and the irreversible Li ions intercalation [41, 42]. After the first cycle, the coulombic efficiency of Ni-BTCEtOH rapidly increases to $96.4\%$ in the $9^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle and then reaches nearly $100\%$ in the $13^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle and maintains at about $100\%$ throughout the following cycles. Among these samples, Ni-BTCEtOH shows the highest initial coulombic efficiency and needs the least cycles to reach a high and stable coulombic efficiency, suggesting an excellent reversibility of lithium storage for the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode.
The charge-discharge profiles of the three samples in various cycles at $200\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ are shown in Fig. 5c, 5d, and A.6a. The first discharge curves of the three samples deliver a similar tendency, which can be divided into three regions (Fig. A.6b). The rapid voltage drops from the open-circuit potential to $1.30{\mathrm{~V}}$ in region A could be assigned to a pseudocapacitive Li ions adsorption. In region B, the voltage decreases from 1.3 to $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ alongside with an evident plateau owing to the formation of the SEI film and irreversible lithiation, corresponding to the irreversible capacity loss in the first CV cycle [43, 44]. In region C, the voltage drop becomes slower below $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ , which mainly comes from the reversible lithiation/delithiation process. During the subsequent cycles, the slope voltage plateaus shift to about $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ for the three samples, which could be assigned to the insertion/extraction process of Li ions into/from the Ni-BTC host. Moreover, the charge-discharge profiles of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode from the $2^{\mathrm{{rd}}}$ to ${100}^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle are similar, suggesting stable electrochemical properties of Ni-BTCEtOH during the successive lithiation/delithiation processes.
The rate performance of the synthesized Ni-BTC MOFs with different morphologies was also investigated through increasing the rate from 100 to $5000{\mathrm{~mA~g}}^{-1}$ (Fig. 6a). The capacity of Ni-BTCEtOH is 937 mA h g-1 at $100\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ . As the current density increases, reversible capacities of 890, 759, 638, 488, and $358~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ are obtained at 200, 500, 1000, 2000, and $5000{\mathrm{~mA~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively, for the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode. The high capacities at high current densities suggest excellent rate performance of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode. When the current density restores to 100 mA $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the $\mathrm{Ni-BTC_{EtOH}}$ electrode retrieves a capacity of $1000\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}.$ , which is close to its original value. With regard to the Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH and Ni-BTCDMF electrodes, reversible capacities of 821 and $737~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are delivered at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ , which decrease rapidly to 130 and $76~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $5000{\mathrm{~mA~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. The corresponding charge-discharge profiles of these three electrodes at various rates are shown in Fig. 6b, 6c, and A.6c. The charge-discharge profiles of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode can maintain well without evident variance even at high rates. For comparison, the charge-discharge profiles of Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH and Ni-BTCDMF begin to appear severe variances at $2000\ \mathrm{mA\g^{-1}}$ . These results all suggest that the rate performance of Ni-BTC could be considerably improved by tuning its morphology. The nanosheets in Ni-BTCEtOH can facilitate the transport of electrons and Li ions at high rates, thus leading to an improved rate capability. Furthermore, EIS was performed to study the charge transfer characteristics of the three electrodes. As shown in Fig. 6d, the semicircle in the high frequency region represents the charge-transfer resistance [45, 46]. After fitting, the value of charge-transfer resistance for the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode is $24.3\Omega$ , which is much lower than that of Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH (127.3 Ω) and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH $(87.9\ \Omega)$ (Table A.1). The relatively low charge-transfer resistance of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode can be beneficial to its electrochemical performance.
Another attractive property of the $\mathrm{Ni-BTC_{EtOH}}$ electrode is its robust long-term cyclability at a high rate of $1000{\mathrm{~mA~g}}^{-1}$ , as shown in Fig. 6e. The capacities of these three electrodes appear an increase tendency in the initial cycles because of the electrochemical activation that has been mentioned above. The capacity of the Ni-BTCDMF electrode increases from 182 to 476 mA h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in the initial 700 cycles and remains stable at this value. As for the Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH electrode, it needs less cycles to reach its maximum value of $631\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (after 630 cycles). For comparison, the flower-like Ni-BTCEtOH electrode only needs 418 cycles to achieve its highest capacity of $956\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mA~h~g^{-1}~}}$ at $1000\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ , which is much higher than that of most MOF-based anode materials reported up to date (Table A.2). These results indicate that the flower-like morphology of Ni-BTCEtOH can largely shorten the diffusion distance of Li ions, and thus the activation process of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode is shorter.
Taking the above results into consideration, the electrochemical performance of the flower-like Ni-BTCEtOH is superior to that of the Ni-BTCDMF and Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH. To directly study the morphology stability of Ni-BTCEtOH during the charge-discharge process, the half-cell of Ni-BTCEtOH was disassembled after 150 cycles at $1000\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ and the morphology of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode was investigated by SEM and TEM. As shown in Fig. 7a, the SEM image shows that the cycled Ni-BTCEtOH still exhibits a well-defined flower-like morphology, without evident structure destruction. The TEM image (Fig. 7b) also clearly shows that the thin nanosheets are still remained after the cycling. These results strongly demonstrate great structural stability of Ni-BTCEtOH, which is responsible for its excellent long-term cycling stability. An illustration of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode is shown in Fig. 7c. According to reported work, the flower-like morphology of Ni-BTCEtOH is beneficial to the strong adhesion between the current collector (i.e., Cu foil) and the electrode material [40]. In addition, the interconnected nanosheets can not only offer connected pathways for electrons but also shorten the diffusion distance of Li ions. Meanwhile, the large surface area of Ni-BTCEtOH ensures a good contact between the active materials and electrolyte. Inside the hierarchical structure of Ni-BTCEtOH, Li ions could be reversibly inserted to/extracted from the Ni-BTCEtOH nanosheets during the discharge/charge process.
To thoroughly investigate the charge-discharge mechanism of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode, XPS, FT-IR, ESR, and CV studies were performed. The XPS measurements of the fresh Ni-BTCEtOH electrode and the electrode discharged to 0.01 V are compared, as shown in Fig. 8a. The Ni 2p XPS spectra of the fresh and discharged Ni-BTCEtOH electrodes nearly overlap. This result demonstrates that Ni in these two electrodes are Ni2+, meaning that $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ does not participate in the reaction as the electrode is discharged to 0.01 V. Therefore, the conversion mechanism may not be applicable for elucidating the insertion-extraction of Li ions in Ni-BTCEtOH. Tarascon group has reported that the lithium-contained conjugated carboxylates, $\mathrm{Li_{2}C_{8}H_{4}O_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , as weak electron-withdrawing ligands, can serve as the sites for the coordination of Li ions with COO and benzene rings, presenting reversible capacities of 300 and 150 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively [47]. Thus, the COO- and benzene rings in Ni-BTCEtOH may play important roles in the charge-discharge process. FT-IR measurements were performed on the Ni-BTCEtOH electrodes at various oxidation states to study the electrochemical reaction process of the electrodes (Fig. 8b). The FT-IR spectra of the fresh and charged Ni-BTCEtOH electrodes are similar and are different from that of the discharged Ni-BTCEtOH electrode. The missing symmetrical stretching vibrations of COO- and C-H in the FT-IR spectrum of the discharged electrode indicates that Li ions either replace the H in C-H and -COOH or insert into the benzene rings. ESR, a powerful analytical technique to study the electronic states, was performed to further explore the reversibility of this process (i.e., Li ions insertion/extraction from the carboxylate groups and benzene rings) in the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode. As shown in Fig. 8c, compared to other two curves, a new peak appears in the blue curve of the discharged Ni-BTCEtOH electrode at $\scriptstyle\mathbf{g}=2.07$ . This new signal at $\mathrm{g}{=}2.07$ can be ascribed to the coordination of Li ions with the benzene rings and carboxylate groups. In addition, the peak at $\mathrm{g}{=}2.00$ is observed, which could be assigned to the free electron from benzene rings [47]. Based on the discussion above, the charge-discharge mechanism of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode could be summarized as follows. Li ions are reversibly inserted to/extracted from the organic moieties (i.e., carboxyl groups and benzene rings) during the discharge/charge process without the direct engagement with $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ . The CV curves of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode at $0.2~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ also agree well with this charge-discharge mechanism (Fig. 8d). The first cycle shows a substantial difference because of the side reactions between the electrode and electrolyte as well as the partially irreversible decomposition of the organic electrolyte to form a SEI layer [48, 49]. After the first CV, a pair of redox peaks (0.85 V/1.41 V) can be observed. Based on the XPS, FT-IR, and ESR results, these redox peaks can tentatively be assigned to the Li ions insertion to/extraction from the carboxylate groups or/and benzene rings (Fig. 7d). In addition, the broad anodic peak possibly suggests the gradual multistep for Li-ion insertion. The enhancement of the exclusion between charges, which is originated from the multistep insertion of Li ions to the conjugated organic moieties, may result in such a phenomenon [25, 30].
## 4. Conclusions
We synthesize hierarchical flower-like Ni-BTCEtOH MOFs through a simple solvothermal method. The formation of the flower-like morphology can be controlled by properly choosing the reaction solvent. Our study suggests that the electrochemical performance of Ni-BTC MOFs as an anode material for LIBs is highly dependent on their morphologies. That is, the Ni-BTCEtOH with the hierarchical flower-like morphology shows a much higher capacity, better rate performance, and superior cyclability compared to Ni-BTCDMF/EtOH and Ni-BTCDMF with a solid spherical morphology. The Ni-BTCEtOH exhibits a capacity of 1085 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ (358 $\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at $5000\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) and shows no obvious capacity decay after 1000 cycles at $1000\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , which is superior to most of the reported MOF-based anode materials. The excellent electrochemical performance of Ni-BTCEtOH is owing to its stable flower-like morphology, large specific surface area (551 m2 g−1), and proper mesoporous structure, which significantly facilitate the transport of Li ions and electrons. In addition, the XPS, FT-IR, ESR, and CV studies demonstrate that the charge-discharge process of the Ni-BTCEtOH electrode follows an intercalation/deintercalation mechanism, which can substantially improve its cycling stability. This work provides an effective strategy to design and fabricate MOF-based LIB anode materials with enhanced electrochemical performance.
